{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778179243,"nonce":"tFG9lWrVES1YCI4Ihejeqf7vdNg=","title":"Post-merge worktree cleanup fails silently: dwt is zsh script, invoked from bash","message":"cleanup_merged_worktrees() in cli/auth-merge.sh (lines 325-350) invokes the dwt cleanup tool via direct execution. dwt is a zsh script (shebang: #!/usr/bin/env zsh), but auth-merge.sh is a bash script. When bash attempts to execute the zsh script, zsh-specific builtins (emulate, print) cause the script to fail immediately. The error is suppressed by `2\u003e/dev/null || true` (line 338), so worktrees are never cleaned up.\n\nResult: after merging feature branches via auth-merge, their corresponding worktrees remain on disk indefinitely.\n\nObserved in rlrmp repo: feature/induced-gain-analyser and feature/peak-forward-velocity-metric merged via auth requests 59f9ea51 and 420a122c (both succeeded), but worktrees at /Users/mll/Main/10 Projects/10 PhD/rlrmp/worktrees/feature__induced-gain-analyser/ and .../feature__peak-forward-velocity-metric/ persist.\n\nFix: either (a) rewrite dwt as a bash script, (b) invoke dwt via zsh -c \"source dwt; ...\", or (c) invoke via exec bash -c to start a new shell for the zsh script.\n\nOption (b) recommended: zsh ensures dwt behaves identically, and the fix is minimal (change line 338 from `\"$dwt\" \"$branch\"` to `zsh \"$dwt\" \"$branch\"`).","files":null}]}